# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — system-wide Flow Search defaults
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   Factory settings for KRunner — the search box that AquariusOS calls "Flow
#   Search". Like its neighbours `kdeglobals` and `kwinrc` in this same folder,
#   everything here is a DEFAULT and never a forced value: the moment a user
#   changes any of it (System Settings → Search → Plasma Search, or → KRunner),
#   their choice is written to their own ~/.config/krunnerrc, which sits ABOVE
#   this file in KDE's search order and wins from then on, permanently.
#
#   The full explanation of that search order — and the warning about the tiny
#   zz-aquarius.sh script that makes this folder count for anything at all — is
#   at the top of `kdeglobals`, right next to this file. Read that one first if
#   any of this is new.
#
# WHY KRUNNER READS THIS FILE AT ALL
#   Two different pieces of KDE read krunnerrc, and it was worth checking BOTH,
#   because they open it in different modes and only one of them is obvious.
#
#     1. The window itself (position, behaviour) — krunner/view.cpp, Plasma/6.7,
#        line 47:
#            m_config = KConfigGroup(KSharedConfig::openConfig(), u"General"_s);
#        `openConfig()` with no arguments means "the application's own rc file,
#        opened in KConfig's fully-cascading mode". The application is krunner,
#        so the file is krunnerrc, and "cascading" means it walks every folder in
#        XDG_CONFIG_DIRS top to bottom, first answer wins — exactly the same way
#        kdeglobals and kwinrc are read.
#        https://invent.kde.org/plasma/plasma-workspace/-/blob/Plasma/6.7/krunner/view.cpp
#
#     2. The list of search plugins ("runners") — KRunner framework,
#        src/runnermanager.cpp:
#            KSharedConfig::openConfig(QStringLiteral("krunnerrc"), KConfig::NoGlobals)
#            ... ->group(QStringLiteral("Plugins"))
#        The flag name `NoGlobals` reads like it might switch the cascade off. It
#        does not, and this is the one genuinely confusing bit in the whole file.
#        In KConfig's own enum, NoGlobals IS CascadeConfig:
#            IncludeGlobals = 0x01,   // blend kdeglobals in
#            CascadeConfig  = 0x02,   // cascade to system-wide config files
#            SimpleConfig   = 0x00,   // just a single file — NO cascade
#            NoGlobals      = CascadeConfig,
#        So `NoGlobals` means "cascade to system settings, but do not blend in
#        the user's kdeglobals". The cascade is ON. Our file is read.
#        https://invent.kde.org/frameworks/kconfig/-/blob/master/src/core/kconfig.h
#        https://invent.kde.org/frameworks/krunner/-/blob/master/src/runnermanager.cpp
#
#   Contrast that with `SimpleConfig`, which is what the keyboard-shortcut daemon
#   uses and which is why our Super-key default had to be shipped from /etc/skel
#   instead of this folder. See the header of
#   ../../../../etc/skel/.config/kglobalshortcutsrc for that story. krunnerrc has
#   no such problem.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE'S SETTINGS SHOW UP
#   Two places, not one — which is a bonus we get for free:
#     - Flow Search itself: Alt+Space (also Alt+F2, and the Search key on
#       keyboards that have one).
#     - The Overview's search box: tap Super. KWin's Overview does not have its
#       own search engine — it embeds `Milou.ResultsView`, which is the same
#       KRunner engine reading this same file.
#       https://invent.kde.org/plasma/kwin/-/blob/Plasma/6.7/src/plugins/overview/qml/Main.qml
#
# WHAT THIS FILE SHIPS
#   Phase 1 of Flow Search: a centred floating palette, and a shorter runner list
#   so nothing in the box teaches command-line syntax or offers a destructive
#   action to somebody who is just typing an app name. The full plain-English
#   write-up — including what phase 2 adds and every honest gap versus the design
#   mock — is in:
#     ../../../../../docs/flow-search.md
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHAPE — a floating palette in the middle of the screen, not a bar at the top
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Out of the box KRunner slides down from the top edge of the screen as a wide
# bar that touches the bezel. The AquariusOS design wants the Spotlight /
# command-palette shape instead: a self-contained rounded card floating over a
# dimmed desktop.
#
# FreeFloating=true is the switch for exactly that. In KRunner's own code it
# removes the slide-down-from-the-top animation, puts a border on all four sides
# instead of three, and re-runs the positioning maths.
#   Source: krunner/view.cpp (Plasma/6.7) line 67 —
#   setFreeFloating(m_config.readEntry("FreeFloating", false))
#   The `false` in that line is KRunner's own default, so this line is doing real
#   work — it is not a no-op restatement.
#
# BEING HONEST ABOUT THE POSITION: the design mock puts the palette 170 pixels
# down a 800-pixel-tall screen. This setting cannot do that, and no setting can.
# When free-floating, KRunner hard-codes the vertical offset to one third of the
# screen height:
#   Source: krunner/view.cpp (Plasma/6.7) margins(), lines 85–91 —
#       if (m_floating) {
#           const QRect r = screen()->availableGeometry();
#           return QMargins({0, r.height() / 3, 0, 0});
#       }
# On a 800-pixel screen that is 266px rather than 170px — a bit lower than drawn.
# There is no config key, no KCM option and no environment variable for it. The
# design's exact number arrives with Flow Search phase 2 (our own window on top
# of KRunner's public engine), and until then this is the closest honest match.
# The width (560px in the mock) is likewise not settable and also waits for
# phase 2.
#
# A person who prefers the top bar can switch it back in System Settings → Search
# → KRunner → "Position on screen".
[General]
FreeFloating=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNNERS — what Flow Search is allowed to search
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The design's promise for this box is one line long: "apps · files · settings ·
# math · actions — one box, no commands to learn." KDE ships roughly twenty
# search plugins ("runners"), and three of them break that promise. Those three
# are switched off below. EVERY OTHER RUNNER IS LEFT COMPLETELY ALONE — this
# section deliberately contains no "…Enabled=true" lines, because writing one
# would freeze a decision that is currently KDE's to make, and KDE's answer is
# already the answer we want.
#
# HOW THE KEY NAME IS BUILT
#   <pluginId>Enabled=false. The plugin id of a C++ runner is not written in its
#   metadata; it is derived from the plugin's filename, which is the name the
#   plugin is built under.
#     Source: KPluginMetaData::pluginId() docs — "For C++ plugins, this ID is
#     derived from the filename. It should not be set in the metadata
#     explicitly."
#     https://invent.kde.org/frameworks/kcoreaddons/-/blob/master/src/lib/plugin/kpluginmetadata.h
#     And the read itself, KPluginMetaData::isEnabled():
#       config.readEntry(pluginId() + QLatin1String("Enabled"), isEnabledByDefault())
#   Each id below was read out of the `kcoreaddons_add_plugin(<target> …
#   INSTALL_NAMESPACE "kf6/krunner")` line in that runner's own CMakeLists.txt,
#   not guessed from the folder name — several of them differ.
#
# HOW A USER TURNS ANY OF THESE BACK ON
#   System Settings → Search → Plasma Search, tick the box next to the name in
#   brackets below. That writes ...Enabled=true into their own
#   ~/.config/krunnerrc, which outranks this file forever after.
[Plugins]

# --- "Command Line" (krunner_shell) -------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT DID: turned whatever you typed into a shell command and offered to run
#   it, with a little "run in terminal" toggle.
# WHY A BEGINNER IS BETTER WITHOUT IT: it is the single largest source of
#   command-line jargon in the box, and it fires on ordinary words. Type `make`,
#   `top` or `mount` while hunting for an app and the top result becomes an
#   invitation to execute a program you have never heard of, with no undo. That
#   is the exact opposite of "no commands to learn". Anyone who wants a shell has
#   Console sitting in the dock.
#   Source: runners/shell/plasma-runner-shell.json (Plasma/6.7) — Name "Command
#   Line", Description "Executes shell commands", EnabledByDefault true.
#   Id from runners/shell/CMakeLists.txt — kcoreaddons_add_plugin(krunner_shell …)
# TURN IT BACK ON: System Settings → Search → Plasma Search → tick "Command Line".
krunner_shellEnabled=false

# --- "Terminate Applications" (krunner_kill) ----------------------------------
# WHAT IT DID: matched running programs by name and offered to force-quit them.
# WHY A BEGINNER IS BETTER WITHOUT IT: it matches on the same words as the
#   ordinary "open this app" result and sorts right next to it. Typing "kdenlive"
#   to open Kdenlive should never put "kill Kdenlive" one arrow-key away — force
#   quitting throws away unsaved work with no confirmation and no undo. Force
#   quitting is still available where it belongs and where it is deliberate:
#   System Monitor, or holding the window's close button.
#   Source: runners/kill/plasma-runner-kill.json (Plasma/6.7) — Name "Terminate
#   Applications", Description "Stop applications that are currently running",
#   EnabledByDefault true.
#   Id from runners/kill/CMakeLists.txt — kcoreaddons_add_plugin(krunner_kill …)
# TURN IT BACK ON: System Settings → Search → Plasma Search → tick "Terminate
#   Applications".
krunner_killEnabled=false

# --- "Special Characters" (krunner_charrunner) --------------------------------
# WHAT IT DID: you type a hexadecimal Unicode code point — `#20AC` — and it hands
#   you the character (€).
# WHY A BEGINNER IS BETTER WITHOUT IT: it is a feature you cannot use without
#   already knowing what a code point is and what the hex number for your symbol
#   is. It is a lookup table you have to memorise, which is the definition of "a
#   command to learn", and its results are meaningless noise to everybody else.
#   The friendly version of the same job already ships: KDE's emoji and symbol
#   picker, Meta+. (Super and the full stop key).
#   Source: kdeplasma-addons runners/characters/plasma-runner-character.json —
#   Name "Special Characters", Description "Creates special characters from their
#   hexadecimal codes", EnabledByDefault true.
#   Id from runners/characters/CMakeLists.txt —
#   kcoreaddons_add_plugin(krunner_charrunner …)
# TURN IT BACK ON: System Settings → Search → Plasma Search → tick "Special
#   Characters".
krunner_charrunnerEnabled=false

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT disabled — the ones we looked hard at and kept
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The research brief listed a few more candidates. Each was checked against what
# the runner actually does in Plasma 6.7, and each survived. Writing the reasons
# down so nobody re-opens these without new information. The full table, with the
# keeps that were never in doubt, is in ../../../../../docs/flow-search.md.
#
# "Sessions" (krunner_sessions) — KEPT, and this one is important.
#   The name makes it sound like it only switches users. It does not: reading
#   runners/sessions/sessionrunner.cpp (Plasma/6.7), this single runner is what
#   produces Log Out, Shut Down, Restart, Lock Screen, Save Session AND Switch
#   User. Shutting the machine down by typing "shut down" IS the design's
#   "actions" pillar. Disabling this would have deleted the feature the mock
#   advertises. Left fully on.
#
# "Web Shortcuts" (krunner_webshortcuts) — KEPT.
#   It does surface `keyword:` syntax, which is why it was on the list. But it is
#   silent until you have already typed a keyword and a colon: it never
#   volunteers itself, never appears while you are typing an app name, and never
#   pushes a result in front of anything. A beginner will simply never meet it,
#   and the person who does type "wiki:aquarius" gets exactly what they asked
#   for. Nothing is gained by removing it and a real convenience is lost.
#
# "Help Runner" (helprunner) — KEPT.
#   It answers `?` and `help` with a list of what the other runners can do. On a
#   first read that looks like "commands to learn" and therefore like something
#   to remove — but it is the opposite: it is the escape hatch that TELLS you the
#   syntax so you never have to memorise any. It only appears when you
#   deliberately ask for it, so it costs a beginner nothing and rescues them once.
#   Source: runners/helprunner/helprunner.json (Plasma/6.7) — "Shows help
#   information about runners when typing \"?\" or \"help\"".
#
# "Konsole Profiles" (krunner_konsoleprofiles) and "Kate Sessions"
#   (krunner_katesessions) — KEPT.
#   Both were considered because their names sound technical. Neither shows any
#   syntax and neither does anything destructive: they list saved profiles and
#   saved sessions the way any other app-launch result does, and they produce no
#   results at all until you have saved one. A beginner who has never opened Kate
#   never sees them.
#
# "Windows" (the `windows` runner, provided by KWin over D-Bus) — KEPT.
#   Finds your open windows and switches to them. That is a plain, useful,
#   non-destructive result and it matches how the Overview already behaves.
#
# Everything else — Applications, File Search (baloosearch), Calculator, Unit
#   Converter, Locations, Places, Recent Files, Bookmarks, System Settings,
#   Software Center, Spell Check, Dictionary, Colours, Date and Time, Power
#   Management — is left exactly as KDE ships it. Those ARE the design's five
#   pillars, and none of them are touched by this file.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT set at all
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keyboard shortcuts — not settable from this folder, and not ours to change in
#   this change anyway. KRunner's default keys (Alt+Space, Alt+F2, and the
#   dedicated Search key) come from its own .desktop file
#   (krunner/org.kde.krunner.desktop.cmake, Plasma/6.7 — X-KDE-Shortcuts), and
#   the daemon that owns shortcuts reads one file in the user's home folder only.
#   See docs/flow-search.md and the /etc/skel kglobalshortcutsrc header.
#
# Window width, corner radius, colours — none of these are krunnerrc settings.
#   The search box's surface comes from the Aquarius Plasma Style's dialogs SVG
#   (Tier 2 track A), which covers KRunner along with every other popup. The
#   width waits for phase 2.
#
#   Blur is not in that list any more either. KRunner used to be drawn as
#   frosted glass; since 2026-08-30 that popup is fully opaque, because the
#   compositor never rendered the blur and see-through-without-frost is at its
#   worst behind a box you are reading and typing into. See
#   docs/plasma-style.md, "Glass removed".
#
# [General] RetainPriorSearch and the history settings — left alone. KRunner's
#   defaults here (remember history, clear the box between openings) are already
#   the behaviour the design assumes, and repeating an existing default is noise
#   unless it is buying insurance against a future change. There is nothing here
#   to insure against.
